      "cleanScript": "There's a tiny bakery in Montreal that smelled like burnt sugar and rain the morning I walked in. No menu boards, no tablet asking for a twenty-five percent tip before you've even said hello. Just a scarred oak counter and a tarnished brass service bell. I stood there for maybe ten seconds, dripping on the tile, and tapped the bell. *Ding.* Out from the back came a man in his seventies, flour dusted up to his elbows, wiping his hands on a striped apron. Here's the thing: she\u2014or rather, the woman ahead of me\u2014had already been standing there when I walked in, and I hadn't even noticed her in the corner. I started to apologize, 'Oh, go ahead, I didn't see you.' The baker didn't look at me. He looked straight at her, didn't look annoyed at all, and said, 'Madame, you were here first. I've been waiting for you to decide.' That's it. No loyalty app. No 'valued customer' script. Just a man who noticed who was in the room before the bell even rang. We spend millions optimizing the checkout flow, and we forget how to look up when someone walks through the door.",
