      "cleanScript": "Elena had rehearsed her Series A pitch forty-two times. Every transition was timed to the second. Every chart was color-coded. She knew the exact moment to click for the hockey-stick graph, and the exact moment to pause for effect. She walked into a dark boardroom on Sand Hill Road, six partners sitting across a long oak table, and she nailed the first nine minutes. She got to slide eleven\u2014the one with the ask, the one she'd spent three weeks perfecting. She raised the clicker. And then the projector bulb popped. Not a flicker. A loud *snap*, a wisp of smoke, and the screen behind her went completely black. The room was pitch dark except for the gray light coming through the blinds. Six partners staring at her. Nobody moved. She didn't apologize. She didn't say, 'Hold on, let me pull it up on my laptop.' She just set the clicker down on the table, walked three steps around the edge of the oak, and stood right in the middle of them. She said, 'I had a really pretty chart for this part. It showed you how we get to ten million in eighteen months.' 'But honestly, you don't need a chart to know if you believe me. We've built the thing nobody else would touch, our customers stay for four years, and you're either in or you're out.' She got the term sheet forty-eight hours later. Not because of the forty-two rehearsals. Because of the thirty seconds after the screen went black.",
