      "annotatedScript": "[SPEED UP] Every Tuesday at 6:45 AM, a retired machinist named Arthur sat in booth nine at Lou's Diner in Cleveland. [SMILE] He ordered two eggs over medium, rye toast dry, and black coffee. He [EMPHASIS: never missed a Tuesday] for eleven years. [BEAT] His waitress, Mara, didn't even carry a pad when he walked in. She just poured the mug before his coat hit the vinyl. [PAUSE 1.5s] One rainy October morning, Arthur came in on time, sat down, and folded his newspaper. Mara set the coffee down. He said, 'Thanks, kid.' Nothing seemed off. [LOWER VOICE] Except when Mara walked back five minutes later with his eggs, Arthur hadn't touched the coffee. Not a sip. And right next to his spoon, he had turned the [EMPHASIS: salt shaker upside down]. [PAUSE 2.0s] Now, in a busy diner, nobody notices a salt shaker. The fry cook is yelling, the bell is dinging, the register is clattering. [LEAN IN] Mara didn't look at the register. She crouched down beside booth nine, level with his eyes, and asked, 'Artie, what's wrong with the coffee?' He looked at her, surprised she'd caught it, and said quietly, 'Nothing's wrong with the coffee, Mara. My wife passed away last night. I just didn't want to sit in an empty house.' [PAUSE 1.5s] [DEADPAN] He [EMPHASIS: didn't say a word] when he walked in. He didn't cry. He didn't make a scene. He just broke a tiny, eleven-year habit\u2014and [EMPHASIS: left a twenty under the saucer] before he even touched his fork. [BEAT] Most of us are running loud diners. We wait for the complaint, the cancellation, the angry email. [LOWER VOICE] By the time someone yells, they've already been gone for weeks.",
