      "annotatedScript": "In a quiet workshop outside Geneva, a watchmaker named Henri spent fourteen months assembling a single tourbillon movement. [BEAT] The piece was already sold for a [EMPHASIS: hundred and eighty thousand dollars]. The case was polished, the velvet box was waiting, and the courier was downstairs. [LOWER VOICE] Henri put his loupe to his eye one last time and held the movement to his ear. [PAUSE 1.5s] Tick. Tick. Tick. [LEAN IN] Then, on the fourth beat, there was a microscopic hesitation\u2014a [EMPHASIS: three-millimeter] misalignment in the escapement wheel. You couldn't see it with the naked eye. A standard timing machine wouldn't even flag it for another six months. [BEAT] His apprentice, watching from the bench, said, 'Henri, the courier is waiting. The client will never notice.' [PAUSE 1.5s] Henri didn't take the loupe off his eye. He reached out, picked up a pair of brass tweezers, and [EMPHASIS: put it in the reject tray]. [DEADPAN] 'You're right,' Henri said. 'The client wouldn't notice. The courier wouldn't notice. [LOWER VOICE] But [EMPHASIS: I would know] every time I walked past this bench.' [PAUSE 1.5s] That's the line between a vendor and a craftsman. A vendor asks, 'Will they catch it?' A craftsman asks, 'Can I live with it?'",
      "cleanScript": "In a quiet workshop outside Geneva, a watchmaker named Henri spent fourteen months assembling a single tourbillon movement. The piece was already sold for a hundred and eighty thousand dollars. The case was polished, the velvet box was waiting, and the courier was downstairs. Henri put his loupe to his eye one last time and held the movement to his ear. Tick. Tick. Tick. Then, on the fourth beat, there was a microscopic hesitation\u2014a three-millimeter misalignment in the escapement wheel. You couldn't see it with the naked eye. A standard timing machine wouldn't even flag it for another six months. His apprentice, watching from the bench, said, 'Henri, the courier is waiting. The client will never notice.' Henri didn't take the loupe off his eye. He reached out, picked up a pair of brass tweezers, and put it in the reject tray. 'You're right,' Henri said. 'The client wouldn't notice. The courier wouldn't notice. But I would know every time I walked past this bench.' That's the line between a vendor and a craftsman. A vendor asks, 'Will they catch it?' A craftsman asks, 'Can I live with it?'",
