      "annotatedScript": "[SPEED UP] Eleven-year-old Maya sat on the wooden bench at her first city recital, her patent-leather shoes barely touching the pedals. [BEAT] Two hundred people in the auditorium. Dead quiet. Her hands hovered over the keys, and she began Debussy's 'Clair de Lune.' [LOWER VOICE] The first twelve bars were magic. Soft, floating, effortless. [SPEED UP] Then her pinky slipped. [EMPHASIS: Loud, ugly F-sharp.] [PAUSE 2.0s] The note echoed off the back wall like a dropped plate. Maya froze. Her shoulders shot up to her ears. She looked at the sheet music, then at the [EMPHASIS: two hundred people] staring back at her. You could feel the panic in the room\u2014everyone waiting for the tears, the bolted exit. [LEAN IN] In the front row, her grandfather didn't gasp. He [EMPHASIS: didn't clap] to be polite. He just leaned forward, rested his elbows on his knees, and softly [EMPHASIS: hummed the next note]. [PAUSE 1.5s] Just one note. Low, steady, in key. [SMILE] Maya blinked. She heard it. Her fingers found the G-flat, and she kept playing. When she finished, the room erupted\u2014not because she was flawless, but because she didn't quit. [LOWER VOICE] When someone on your team hits the wrong note in front of everyone, they don't need a lecture on music theory. They just need someone in the front row to hum the next note.",
      "cleanScript": "Eleven-year-old Maya sat on the wooden bench at her first city recital, her patent-leather shoes barely touching the pedals. Two hundred people in the auditorium. Dead quiet. Her hands hovered over the keys, and she began Debussy's 'Clair de Lune.' The first twelve bars were magic. Soft, floating, effortless. Then her pinky slipped. Loud, ugly F-sharp. The note echoed off the back wall like a dropped plate. Maya froze. Her shoulders shot up to her ears. She looked at the sheet music, then at the two hundred people staring back at her. You could feel the panic in the room\u2014everyone waiting for the tears, the bolted exit. In the front row, her grandfather didn't gasp. He didn't clap to be polite. He just leaned forward, rested his elbows on his knees, and softly hummed the next note. Just one note. Low, steady, in key. Maya blinked. She heard it. Her fingers found the G-flat, and she kept playing. When she finished, the room erupted\u2014not because she was flawless, but because she didn't quit. When someone on your team hits the wrong note in front of everyone, they don't need a lecture on music theory. They just need someone in the front row to hum the next note.",
