      "annotatedScript": "It was [EMPHASIS: twenty below zero] in Duluth, Minnesota, and a regional VP named Marcus was stranded in a hotel parking lot with a dead rental car and a flight to catch in forty minutes. [SPEED UP] When the tow truck finally rolled up, Marcus was already pacing by the hood, jumper cables in hand. 'Just [EMPHASIS: hook up the cables],' he yelled over the wind. 'I've got a board meeting in Chicago!' [BEAT] The driver, an older guy named Dale, stepped out, zipped his Carhartt up to his chin, and [EMPHASIS: didn't touch the battery]. [PAUSE 1.5s] He knelt in the snow with a flashlight and stared under the bumper for thirty seconds. Marcus snapped, 'Hey! We're burning daylight!' [DEADPAN] Dale stood up, clicked off his flashlight, and said, 'Your oil pan is frozen solid, buddy. You crank this engine right now, you'll [EMPHASIS: cracked the block] in three seconds. Then you're not missing a flight\u2014you're buying a new car.' [PAUSE 1.5s] [LOWER VOICE] Marcus stood there in the snow, cables dangling from his gloves, realizing how close he'd come to turning a twenty-minute delay into a total loss. [LEAN IN] Every organization has a Marcus. When things stall, we scream for more voltage. More meetings, more budget, more pressure. [BEAT] Sometimes the smartest person in the room is the one who refuses to turn the key until they look underneath.",
