      "cleanScript": "Three years ago in Lisbon, I got tired of the tourist crowds and ducked down a steep cobblestone alley that wasn't on my map. Halfway down, behind an unmarked wooden door, I heard the soft clink of ceramic. I peeked inside. It was a tiny workshop, no bigger than a hotel bathroom, smelling of wet clay and turpentine. An old artisan was sitting at a scarred oak table, surrounded by hundreds of hand-painted blue tiles. On his left was a pile of broken ones\u2014chipped corners, hairline fractures, kiln mistakes. I watched him pick up a cracked tile. He wasn't throwing them away. He took a fine brush, dipped it in liquid gold leaf, and carefully traced the jagged fracture from edge to edge. I asked him, 'Why spend all that time on the broken ones? Nobody buys damaged tile.' He didn't even look up from his brush. 'The perfect ones,' he said, 'go on the back wall where nobody looks. The ones with the gold? That's the part people remember.' We spend half our careers trying to sand down our scars so we look like everyone else's perfect tile. Nobody remembers smooth. They remember where it broke\u2014and what you used to put it back together.",
