      "annotatedScript": "In November of 1998, a tugboat captain named Arthur Pendelton was easing a sixty-foot harbor tug into Pier 7 in Halifax. [LOWER VOICE] The fog was so thick it tasted like cold salt, and you couldn't see the bowsprit from the wheelhouse. Inside, though, the new twenty-four-year-old mate was beaming. He had three glowing radar screens, a brand-new depth sounder, and a GPS plotter that cost more than Arthur's truck. [SMILE] 'Cap,' the kid said, tapping the glass, 'we're dead center in the channel. [EMPHASIS: All green on the screen.]' [PAUSE 1.5s] Arthur didn't answer. He reached into his oilskin pocket, pulled out a cardboard matchbook from a diner in Dartmouth, and tried to strike a match. Nothing. The sulfur head just smeared into gray paste. He [EMPHASIS: stepped outside] onto the freezing wing bridge, leaned over the rail, and sniffed the air. [BEAT] Then he threw the twin engines into full reverse so hard the coffee mugs flew off the chart table. [SPEED UP] 'What are you doing?' the mate yelled. 'The screen says we have forty feet of clearance!' Arthur pointed into the white wall ten feet off their starboard hull. Out of the mist, the black, barnacle-crusted pilings of the old coal wharf loomed like teeth. [LOWER VOICE] 'Kid,' Arthur said, wiping spray off his glasses, 'your screen is measuring where the satellite thinks we are. I could [EMPHASIS: smell the creosote] on those pilings.' [PAUSE 2s] He tossed the soggy matchbook onto the console. [DEADPAN] 'If the matches in my pocket are wet, the fog is inside the boat. [EMPHASIS: Never trust a dry instrument] when you can't see the pier.'",
