      "annotatedScript": "In a narrow workshop behind Old Town Square in Prague, a twenty-year-old apprentice named Tomas spent [EMPHASIS: six months] doing nothing but sweeping brass shavings. He wanted to oil the escapements on nineteenth-century pocket watches. He wanted to do the real work. Every morning, his master, Herr Novak, would sit at the bench, loupe in his eye, and never even look up. [BEAT] Finally, on a rainy Tuesday, Tomas snapped. 'Master Novak,' he said, 'I know the gear ratios. I've memorized every spring. When do I get to oil a movement?' Novak slowly unscrewed the brass loupe from his eye. He didn't reach for the oil can. Instead, [EMPHASIS: he handed him a piece of chalk]. [PAUSE 1.5s] 'Take off your jacket,' Novak said. 'Draw a circle on your left sleeve.' Tomas thought the old man had lost his mind, but he did it. 'Now,' Novak said, 'put your elbow on the bench, close your eyes, and tell me what you hear.' Tomas listened. Rain on the cobblestones. A tram bell two streets over. The faint, uneven tick of three clocks on the wall. [LOWER VOICE] 'Good,' Novak whispered. 'Now keep your eyes closed for twenty minutes. Every time your sleeve touches the bench, the chalk will remind you that you moved.' [LEAN IN] Tomas sat there in the dark, shoulders burning, until the tram faded and he could [EMPHASIS: hear the room] breathing. [PAUSE 2s] Novak tapped the bench once. 'A watch doesn't break because you lack skill, Tomas. It breaks because you're in a hurry. Learn to sit still [EMPHASIS: before you touch the oil].'",
      "cleanScript": "In a narrow workshop behind Old Town Square in Prague, a twenty-year-old apprentice named Tomas spent six months doing nothing but sweeping brass shavings. He wanted to oil the escapements on nineteenth-century pocket watches. He wanted to do the real work. Every morning, his master, Herr Novak, would sit at the bench, loupe in his eye, and never even look up. Finally, on a rainy Tuesday, Tomas snapped. 'Master Novak,' he said, 'I know the gear ratios. I've memorized every spring. When do I get to oil a movement?' Novak slowly unscrewed the brass loupe from his eye. He didn't reach for the oil can. Instead, he handed him a piece of chalk. 'Take off your jacket,' Novak said. 'Draw a circle on your left sleeve.' Tomas thought the old man had lost his mind, but he did it. 'Now,' Novak said, 'put your elbow on the bench, close your eyes, and tell me what you hear.' Tomas listened. Rain on the cobblestones. A tram bell two streets over. The faint, uneven tick of three clocks on the wall. 'Good,' Novak whispered. 'Now keep your eyes closed for twenty minutes. Every time your sleeve touches the bench, the chalk will remind you that you moved.' Tomas sat there in the dark, shoulders burning, until the tram faded and he could hear the room breathing. Novak tapped the bench once. 'A watch doesn't break because you lack skill, Tomas. It breaks because you're in a hurry. Learn to sit still before you touch the oil.'",
